## 6. DESIGN DIRECTION — THE NEW JAYSTARBLISS DESIGN SYSTEM

Create a cohesive, restrained, modern education SaaS design with clear information hierarchy, excellent spacing, quick navigation and a strong sense of trust.

It should feel like a deliberately designed product, not a collection of independently styled pages.

### Brand and visual language

Use the existing Jaystarbliss identity as the starting point:

- Brand navy/slate: `#1E293B`
- Brand red: `#B91C1C`
- Neutral background: `#F8FAFC`
- White/light surfaces with clear borders.
- Existing accessible blue/focus accents where they fit the current theme.

Keep the red as a meaningful brand and action accent, not an indiscriminate colour for every card, badge and icon. Use restrained semantic colours for statuses and alerts. Avoid introducing a completely different purple-heavy or rainbow dashboard palette.

Review the existing `src/uiux-pro-max.css`, `src/index.css`, `src/components/portal/portal-polish.css`, Tailwind configuration and other stylesheets. Identify duplicate tokens, contradictory global overrides and overlapping visual treatments before changing them.

Reduce excessive glassmorphism, heavy backdrop filters, decorative gradients, large shadows, decorative backgrounds, redundant card borders and unnecessary animations where they obscure content or reduce readability.

Prefer subtle elevation, consistent spacing, neutral surfaces, crisp borders, predictable components and typography that remains readable on smaller screens.

Maintain functional light mode, dark mode and existing high-contrast behaviour. Do not introduce a design token that is readable in light mode but disappears in dark mode.

### Establish reusable design tokens

Create or consolidate a documented source of truth for:

- Colour and semantic status tokens.
- Typography, font weights, heading scale and line heights.
- Spacing scale and container widths.
- Border widths and radii.
- Elevation and shadows.
- Control heights and touch-target sizes.
- Responsive breakpoints and content grids.
- Focus, hover, selected, loading, disabled and error states.
- Z-index layers for sticky headers, overlays, drawers, dropdowns and modals.
- Motion duration and reduced-motion behaviour.

Use CSS variables and existing Tailwind conventions where possible. Do not build a parallel design system that conflicts with the existing theme engine.

### Shared component foundation

Audit existing components first, then standardise or introduce reusable variants for:

- Compact page header.
- Main content container.
- Primary, secondary, quiet and destructive buttons.
- Inputs, selects, text areas, checkboxes, radio controls and validation messages.
- Searchable and filterable selectors.
- Status badges and contextual alerts.
- Cards and compact metric summaries.
- Data tables, mobile row layouts, pagination, filters and row actions.
- Accessible tabs and segmented controls.
- Modals, drawers, popovers and confirmation dialogs.
- Empty states, loading skeletons, inline errors, retry actions and success feedback.
- File upload, image upload, question editor and preview states.
- Notification lists, activity feeds and audit log rows.
- Charts, labels and data legends.
- Reports, printable summaries and download actions.
- Tooltips and keyboard-focus treatments.

Components must have predictable props, semantic HTML and documented variants. Avoid creating separate near-identical buttons or card components for each portal.

### Card and information hierarchy

Do not put every section inside a separate large rounded rectangle. Use a combination of open page sections, compact panels, clear dividers, data tables and occasional cards when each card genuinely represents a distinct object or task.

Metric cards should only be used when the metric supports a decision or a useful glance. Do not manufacture metrics, fake trends or decorative numbers.

Avoid duplicated page titles, greetings, dates, lengthy subtitles and brand slogans above the same content. Use a concise title and put meaningful instructions close to the workflow that needs them.

Use consistent sentence case for content headings and simple, action-focused labels.

## 7. SHARED NAVIGATION AND PAGE STRUCTURE

### Compact sticky headers

Redesign both `PortalLayout.tsx` and `AdminLayout.tsx` so that every authenticated dashboard page has one compact, consistent header that stays at the top when scrolling.

The principal visible text in the header should be the **current page title**. Eliminate redundant “Workspace”, institute name, long greeting, role description and repeated title-banner text from the page-heading area.

Retain important controls such as menu toggles, notifications, profile access, theme control, global search and admin-only utilities where appropriate. Use compact icon actions with accessible labels and tooltips when suitable.

Ensure the header is truly sticky within the application's actual scroll container. Avoid duplicate sticky headers, stacked topbars or headings that cover focused controls.

Remove the visible email-verification notification banner across all authenticated portals, including equivalent banners in separate layouts if they exist. This is a UI request only: do not remove authentication checks, verification support in the backend, account enforcement or any unrelated security mechanism.

### Adaptive desktop and mobile navigation

Use a responsive navigation layout based on the available viewport and user role.

On desktop, provide a stable sidebar or collapsible navigation rail with concise labels, clear active states, predictable grouping and appropriate tooltips.

On tablet, adapt the sidebar width and content density so the main content retains usable space.

On mobile:

- Use a compact topbar.
- Allow the navigation drawer to open with a button.
- Support swipe-right from the left edge to open it and swipe-left to close it, where safe and appropriate.
- Ensure gestures do not interfere with vertical scrolling, text selection, inputs or horizontal data content.
- Keep a backdrop and close action.
- Close the drawer after navigation when appropriate.
- Consider a compact bottom navigation for a small number of high-frequency user actions, but do not make the menu unnecessarily complex or duplicate every sidebar item at the bottom.
- Preserve access to less-frequently-used sections such as settings and support.

Navigation items must reflect actual permissions. A hidden or visible menu item is never the authority for backend access.

### Route-aware page titles

Map the current location to the correct title. Include nested and dynamic routes such as individual student details, examination attempts, create/edit forms, billing details, report views and CMS editing.

Do not show “Dashboard” as the title when the user is working on a specific examination, report or account detail.

Remove duplicate page headings when the shared header already communicates the page title. Keep section-level headings within the page when they help users understand the content.

