## 10. EXAMINATION AND QUESTION AUTHORING DETAILS

The redesign must include the full existing examination ecosystem.

Provide a clean exam-management table or list, strong search and status filtering, clear creation actions, safe editing and no accidental duplication.

Native examinations must support the existing question types and validated settings. Where the current schema and grading engine support them, provide accessible question cards, answer choices, correct answers, point values, required flags, answer feedback, question reordering, duplication and deletion.

Use a clearly organised Import Questions workflow for the supported sources, such as formatted text, Excel/CSV and the authorised question bank. Show a preview, identify invalid questions and confirm before appending questions to the current draft.

Never include demo or fabricated questions as default data. New exams should start empty unless the tutor explicitly imports or duplicates existing content.

External-link examinations must remain distinct from native assessments. They should use the appropriate access code, external URL and assignment settings, without displaying native response analytics or falsely claiming that a third-party provider's responses are tracked.

Keep native grading, timing, attempts, manual result release and historical attempt snapshots consistent with the existing backend.

Ensure question-editing features do not inadvertently invalidate answers or marks from attempts already submitted. Do not recreate an examination record during ordinary editing or republishing.

## 11. SCHOOL IDENTITY, GRADING AND DATA CONSISTENCY

Ensure every report uses the correct actual partner-school name resolved through its authorised school record. Do not substitute the platform name for a partner school's name.

Use a consistent authoritative scoring model. One mark out of three must remain 1/3, not 1/100. The percentage must be derived from the actual total possible marks, and the same authoritative result must appear across the relevant roles.

Preserve school boundaries in all queries and exports. A redesigned table, search control or filter must not broaden the records a user can access.

Do not invent missing scores or display fabricated dashboard statistics. If a real record is missing, show a clear and useful empty/unavailable state.

Use actual application records for dashboards, charts, notifications, counts and report exports.

## 12. RESPONSIVE DESIGN REQUIREMENTS

Design mobile-first, then adapt for medium and wide screens. Do not shrink the desktop design proportionally and call it responsive.

Test at representative widths such as 320, 360, 390, 430, 768, 1024, 1280 and 1440 CSS pixels.

### Mobile requirements

- No unintentional horizontal page overflow.
- No clipped buttons, inputs, headings or important data.
- No text that requires zooming to read.
- No action hidden beyond the viewport.
- No fixed-width form layout that breaks on narrow screens.
- No full desktop table that becomes unusable on a phone.
- No mobile modals that extend beyond the viewport.
- No popovers positioned off-screen.
- No overlapping sticky headers, footers, floating widgets or navigation drawers.
- No keyboard-obscured form controls.
- No accidental scroll locking that traps users.
- No touch-only operations without equivalent accessible controls.

For data tables, retain table semantics where appropriate. On narrow screens, use a well-designed responsive row/card presentation or deliberate table scrolling where the data genuinely requires two dimensions. Keep important row identifiers visible and provide accessible ways to access hidden columns and actions.

For dense forms, use one clear column on phones and a sensible multi-column layout on wider screens. Preserve logical reading order and field grouping.

Do not make every card taller, stack every decorative panel or replace a usable table with dozens of unrelated cards. Choose layouts according to the actual task.

### Desktop and tablet requirements

Use available screen space intelligently. Keep content at a readable maximum width, allow data-heavy tables to use adequate width, and do not centre narrow content in a sea of unused space.

Use collapsible side navigation where appropriate, but keep the main content width stable and prevent sidebar animations from making the whole page jump.

On tablet, adapt the layout based on available width and actual interaction requirements, not simply device type.

