Implement semantic buttons, links, headings, forms and tables. Provide visible focus indicators and ensure sticky elements do not obscure keyboard focus.

Use keyboard-operable menus, dialogs, tabs, forms and data tables. Use correct ARIA roles, states and relationships when they are needed; do not add ARIA roles as decoration.

Dialogs should manage focus appropriately, close predictably, support Escape when appropriate and return focus to the trigger. Tabs should have proper tab semantics and keyboard behaviour. Disclosures should expose their expanded state.

Do not rely on colour alone to indicate success, errors, role, active state or status.

Aim for comfortably tappable controls—preferably around 44×44 CSS pixels for common touch controls, while ensuring WCAG 2.2 minimum target-size and spacing requirements are met.

Respect reduced-motion preferences. Keep animations subtle and purposeful. Avoid background movement, excessive hover transforms, large parallax effects and animations that slow down routine tasks.

Preserve screen-reader labels for icon-only actions. Avoid placeholder-only forms. Place validation messages next to the related inputs and use useful error language.

## 14. PERFORMANCE AND VISUAL STABILITY

The new UI should feel faster as well as look better.

Preserve route-level lazy loading where suitable. Avoid adding large dependencies for small visual improvements.

Avoid unnecessary rerenders, duplicate Firestore listeners, repeated unrestricted collection reads, expensive animations and oversized image assets.

Keep charts responsive and legible. Prevent layout shifts caused by delayed fonts, images, tables or changing card heights.

Where browser performance tooling is available, measure and improve Core Web Vitals and actual interaction responsiveness. Use these as reference targets, not as claims without measurement:

- LCP at or below 2.5 seconds where achievable.
- INP below 200 milliseconds where measurable.
- CLS below 0.1 where measurable.

Preserve all real-time updates and data-refresh behaviour. Do not improve performance by silently removing features or reducing necessary data accuracy.

## 15. IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY — DO NOT REDESIGN EVERYTHING BLINDLY IN ONE PASS

Work systematically and keep the existing application working throughout the process.

### Phase A — Baseline and inventory

Produce the route/role matrix and feature-preservation matrix. Identify the main layout shells, shared design primitives, conflicting style layers and highest-risk workflows.

Record current behaviour before making changes.

### Phase B — Design-system foundation

Define the common tokens and standardise the reusable controls, typography, layout containers, compact sticky headers, tables, forms, status indicators, notifications, feedback states, navigation and responsive containers.

Do not delete old CSS blindly. Identify which rules are still used and migrate them carefully.

### Phase C — Shared application shell

Redesign `PortalLayout` and `AdminLayout`, including navigation, compact title headers, responsive sidebar/drawer behaviour, profile and notification controls, theming, focus management and sticky-scroll behaviour.

Apply the title/header rules consistently across routes. Remove redundant in-page title banners only where they duplicate the shared header, and preserve meaningful section headings and form instructions.

Remove the visible email-verification notification banner across the authenticated portals without changing the underlying auth/security model.

### Phase D — Redesign role-specific pages

Implement each role's dashboard and its subpages. Work in logical groups and verify each group before moving to the next.

Suggested groups:

1. Admin dashboard and system management.
2. Admin CMS and content-management workflows.
3. Users, roles, onboarding, approvals and school administration.
4. Tutor/staff dashboards, students, schedules, resources and exams.
5. Student dashboards, coursework, examinations and learning tools.
6. Parent dashboard, linked-child progress, results and payments.
7. School dashboard, roster, upcoming exams, history, access codes and reports.
8. Shared finance, billing, settings, support and resource modules.

Do not stop after the main dashboard. Continue through all relevant page routes, forms, modal states, tab states, edit flows, error states and responsive layouts.

### Phase E — Workflow-level integration

Verify each UI control remains connected to the correct state, persisted record, API endpoint and backend permission. Remove fake or dead controls. Repair broken integrations discovered during implementation.

Do not change API semantics simply to accommodate a redesigned component.

### Phase F — Regression, accessibility and responsive QA

Test the complete route inventory, feature checklist and role matrix. Compare the redesigned UI to the baseline and confirm that no workflow has disappeared.

Fix defects introduced by the redesign before declaring completion.

## 16. VISUAL QA AND SCREENSHOT PROTOCOL

For every audited route, capture or review the page at representative desktop, tablet and mobile sizes where tooling permits.

At minimum, test every major workflow in its meaningful states:

- Initial/loading.
- Normal populated state.
- Empty state.
- Error state.
- Form validation.
- Dialog/modal open and close.
- Menu or drawer open and closed.
- Read-only versus editable view.
- Loading/saving feedback.
- Successful completion.
- Permission-denied state where relevant.
- Long text, long names, large numbers and large data tables.
- Light, dark and high-contrast themes where supported.

Pay particular attention to examination creation, question imports, student attempts, result-release controls, school reports, user-role updates, approvals, payment workflows, bulk exports and dangerous admin actions.

Use automated screenshot comparison or visual regression tools if already available or easily supported. Do not add substantial dependencies solely for a superficial test.

Document which pages were actually visually inspected and which were not due to environment limitations.

